🪨 BLOCK 1 — PHYSICAL OBSERVATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Michael reported that the press-and-hold precision loupe did not align truthfully on
  physical glass.
- The post or handle appeared to remain beneath his finger instead of appearing at the
  expected location in the magnified window.
- The loupe crosshair and working coordinate appeared misaligned.
- This observation blocks RW-TX-016-PMP Test 2 and prevents field acceptance of 1.2.0.
- No claim is made that every device or placement workflow failed in the same way.

🪨 BLOCK 2 — REPOSITORY FINDING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- The original PrecisionLoupe attached Android Magnifier to the MapView container even
  though MapLibre renders through a child SurfaceView or TextureView.
- The original loupe always magnified the raw finger coordinate while gates, shared
  corners, and paddock endpoints could accept a different snapped coordinate.
- The original window used Android's default offset and did not independently place the
  loupe relative to the finger and available screen space.
- MapLibre overlay updates are asynchronous, so a moved handle could be one rendered
  frame behind the magnified content.
- These are code-supported causes consistent with Michael's observation; only physical
  retesting can prove that the correction resolves the field behavior.

🎮 BLOCK 3 — CORRECTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Corrected source b28ed7c:
- locates and magnifies MapLibre's actual SurfaceView or TextureView render child;
- distinguishes the crosshair source coordinate from the finger-controlled window position;
- resolves valid gate, shared-corner, and paddock-boundary snapping before display;
- uses that displayed coordinate for release acceptance;
- positions the loupe above and horizontally away from the finger where screen space allows;
- clamps the loupe window to visible map bounds; and
- refreshes the magnifier on the next display frame so moving overlays remain visible.

